https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/7b84159406041ddd38658b2ea07058cbb8a0f20a/src/Middleware/Session/src/SessionMiddleware.cs#L157

Oh I see! The value for cookie is created but the cookie itself is only added to the response if `Session.Set()` is called. 
I'll open another issue to talk about if the extra allocation of a byte[] and two strings could be prevented.

>  it can be re-used across multiple sessions

But are those sessions for the same cookie value and in the same browser? If so in the context of request correlation that's not so bad.

- How about only create a session scope if sessionKey is available in the cookie? A major downside would be that the first request does not contains the scope.

- Or Introduce a new `ISession.IsLoaded` property and pass `ISession` into the log scope and only return session id if `IsLoaded` is true.


- At this point I think I don't really care about session! A simple protected cookie value would also do the trick. Any Idea?
```csharp
public class CorrelationScopeMiddleware
{
    public async Task Invoke(HttpContext context)
    {
        var cookieValue = context.Request.Cookies[_options.Cookie.Name!];
        var correlationValue = CookieProtection.Unprotect(_dataProtector, cookieValue, _logger);

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(correlationValue)
        {
            var guidBytes = new byte[16];
            RandomNumberGenerator.Fill(guidBytes);
            correlationValue = new Guid(guidBytes).ToString();
            cookieValue = CookieProtection.Protect(_dataProtector, correlationValue);
            context.Response.OnStarting(SetCookie, state: (this, cookieValue))
        }

        using var scope = new CorrelationScope(correlationValue)
        await _next(context);
    }
}

```
